\f0\fs24 \cf0 Regarding the Linotype file format:\
\
Most lines in the file represents one instruction to the machine, being a keypress, sending a line, going to bold,\'85 Each line providing an instruction is marked as \'91key\'92. If the line means something else, it should contain a tag like \'91warming\'92, \'91info\'92, etc.\
\
Each line is structured like: \
	\cf2 key <name> <key number on the keyboard> \{thickness\} [metadata]\cf0 \
For example:\
	\cf2 key i 64 \{1.61\} [replacing \'ef] [orig:auto] \cf0 \
for a normal letter, or \
	\cf2 key wedge_space 129 \{1-2.5\} [orig:auto] \cf0 \
for a wedge space with a variable thickness, or\
	\cf2 key line_ending 130 \{\} [line_min_length:150] [line_max_length:166] [orig:auto] \cf0 \
for an instruction to the machine, such as sending a line, or changing to bold,\'85\
\
Keyboard differ per country or per diagram. It is important to follow the keyboard layouts from the specific machine. Keys that are determined by the app:\
	\cf2 0: a hand matrix or an error. The app should notify the user about it (or solve it automatically if possible). A sound should be made, if possible.\
	1-128: the possible number of keys on a machine.\
	129: a wedge space, with a variable thickness\
	130: sending a line to the casting\
\cf3 \
Common metadata (but it can vary):\
	\cf2 [orig:auto] \cf3 this entry was generated by an application\
	\cf2 [replacing \'ef]\cf3  this entry was replaced. This tag allows an app to notify a user that replacements have been done.\
\
Comments can be added to the file by a hashtag. Each file starts with at least three comments:\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf2 	# Linotype text\
	# Format version: 1\
	# Generated by: Linotype Computer Connection\
	# All units in cicero\
	# Line length: 8.0 cicero\
	# Wedge space thickness: 1-2.5\cf3 \
\
